NAME
    pkg - create, inspect, verify, migrate, patch, and archive game packages

SYNOPSIS
    pkg make <path>... [--self-contained] [--zip]
    pkg ls [<console>] [--all]
    pkg check [<package>...]
    pkg migrate <package>...
    pkg patch add <package> <file>
    pkg patch rm <package> <n>
    pkg zip <package>...
    pkg identity <package> original|patched
    pkg wait [pkg<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    Manages game packages for the emusen.library module. A game package is
    a directory under /Packages/<console>/ containing package.json and the
    game's per-title stores: battery saves in saves/, save states and
    thumbnails in states/, cheats in cheats.json, per-game settings in
    settings.json, artwork in media/, and optional soft patches.

    A linked package (the default) references the original game inside the
    library (AppSettings.RomDirectory) by path, size, timestamp, and
    CRC-32/MD5/SHA-1 without copying ROM bytes. A self-contained package
    copies the game and all companion files (cue tracks, m3u discs, or
    converted CHDs) into the package folder so it is fully portable. The
    library itself is strictly read-only.

    Once a package claims a game (and its companions or converted disc
    originals), opening either the package or the original library path
    uses the package's saves, states, settings, art, and patches. If the
    library file moves, EmuSen relocates it by size and hash and updates
    the manifest. If the library file's bytes change, opening the plain
    path treats it as an unlinked game while opening the package refuses
    with both hashes reported.

OPTIONS
    make <path>... [--self-contained] [--zip]
        Create packages under /Packages/<console>/ for games under each
        library-relative <path> ('/' for the whole library). With
        --self-contained, copy game files into the package (upgrading
        linked packages in place). With --zip (implies --self-contained),
        also write /Packages/<console>/<name>.zip if not already present.

    ls [<console>] [--all]
        List packages (optionally filtered by console name, ES-DE id, or
        Packages folder) with mode, state (ok, changed, moved, missing,
        refused), and sizes. With --all, also list unclaimed library games
        and mark discs eligible for chd(1).

    check [<package>...]
        Verify each <package> (relative to /Packages, or a package zip) by
        hashing the game, companion files, and any soft patches against
        the manifest. Relocates moved library files and refreshes changed
        timestamps. Unpacks zips into a temporary folder for verification
        and removes it afterward.

    migrate <package>...
        Copy existing battery saves, per-game save media, save states and
        state records, cheat lists, per-game settings and crops, and
        artwork from /Media/<system>/<type>/ into <package> without
        overwriting files already present or modifying originals. Shared
        memory cards, console memory, play time, favourites, and scraped
        metadata are left in place.

    patch add <package> <file>
        Validate an IPS, UPS, or BPS soft patch at shell path <file>, copy
        it into <package>, append it to the patch chain, and record the
        patched image hashes. UPS and BPS source checksums are verified;
        IPS patches are pinned to the source SHA-1. Disc games are not
        supported; patched images are capped at 64 MiB.

    patch rm <package> <n>
        Remove patch <n> (1-based index from 'pkg ls') from <package>.
        Refused if subsequent patches would fail to apply without it.

    zip <package>...
        Write a self-contained <package> (including saves and states) to a
        zip archive beside its folder. Linked packages are refused. Zip
        archives support up to 65,534 files and 4 GiB (no Zip64) and store
        already-compressed files without recompression.

    identity <package> original|patched
        Choose whether the scraper queries ScreenScraper using the
        original dump hashes ('original') or the patched image hashes
        first ('patched'). Refused on packages with no soft patches.

    wait [pkg<n>]
        Follow a background pkg job until completion.

SOFT PATCHES
    Opening a patched package validates every file and patch SHA-1 before
    starting the core; any mismatch refuses launch rather than running a
    corrupt image. Patched games store battery saves, save media, and
    states separately under <name>.patched-<key> (where <key> is 8 hex
    digits derived from the ordered patch list) so original saves remain
    untouched.

PACKAGE ZIPS
    Opening a package zip unpacks it into /Packages once, preserving file
    timestamps, and launches the extracted folder. Subsequent launches
    open the existing folder and never overwrite it from the zip.

JOBS AND LOGS
    Long-running commands continue in the background as pkg<n>. Use ps(1)
    to inspect progress, 'pkg wait' to reattach, and kill(1) to stop.
    Outcomes from 'make', 'check', and 'migrate' are logged to
    /Logs/pkg_<day>.log.

EXAMPLES
    pkg make nes
    pkg make "snes/Super Metroid (USA).sfc"
    pkg make --self-contained --zip "snes/Super Metroid (USA).sfc"
    pkg check "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc.zip"
    pkg zip "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc"
    pkg ls NES
    pkg ls --all PlayStation
    pkg check NES
    pkg migrate "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc"
    pkg patch add "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc" /Patches/Redesign.bps
    pkg patch rm "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc" 1
    pkg identity "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc" patched
    pkg wait pkg1

EXIT STATUS
    0   Command succeeded (or detached cleanly to a background job).
    1   Invalid arguments, path/package error, patch mismatch, or job
        failure.

SEE ALSO
    chd(1), rom(1), scrape(1), ps(1), kill(1), hier(7)
